Several observations can be made from Table~\ref{tab:results}.
\begin{itemize}\vspace{-1mm}
\item Comparing setting~1 (i.e., the baseline model) and setting 2, where the number of voters increases from 1 to 100, the model performance improves for all test sets owing to the soft voting as described in Section~\ref{subsec:soft-voting}.
\item Comparing settings~2 and 3, where we use raw features verses 20 most variant PCA transformed features, all but one test set show improvement of the model performance introduced by PCA transform and subset selection as described in Section~\ref{subsec:PCA}.
\item Comparing settings~3 and 4, where the PCA transformed features are selected in the conventional way verses SRS, most test sets show improvement of the model performance introduced by SRS as described in Section~\ref{subsec:smartPC}.
\end{itemize}
\vspace{-1mm}

Similarly, by comparing the last row of Table~\ref{tab:results}, where all testing samples are taken into consideration, we can confirm the improvements of overall performance by virtue of each component in our model introduced in Section~\ref{sec:model}. 
Fig.~\ref{fig:roc-prc-curves} shows the corresponding ROC curve and precision-recall curve, where the performance improvements introduced by each component of the model can be clearly observed as the gaps between lines.

Model training (with setting~4) takes up to $3$ hours with our hardware devices, and prediction takes less than a minute per test set, much less than the time required for detailed routing.

